  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Switchboard Panel",
    "overview": "The console is the floor's switchboard as drawn by the electrician who wired it. The floor is a single-line diagram (transformer, meter, main breaker, 415 V bus, one feeder per room), and every other surface borrows the same vocabulary: seams instead of cards, lamps instead of badges, line weight instead of decoration. It comes in two lights, chosen from a Light/Dark switch in the top bar and remembered per browser: **Daylight** (the default), light content framed by a navy rail and top bar, and **Dusk**, a slate-dark panel for low light. Both themes are one set of roles; only the values change.\n\nColour is functional, never decorative, but the console is no longer monochrome. One blue accent carries action and selection; one hue per load type (AC, fans, lights, AV) follows that type everywhere it appears; room types tint the floor plan and departments tint the timetable. Amber still means one thing only: power flowing into a room the timetable says is empty. Red and green are still panel lamps.\n\nDensity is high and calm. Panels are split by 1px seams and sit flush; nothing floats, glows, or fades. Words are set in Archivo, every figure in tabular JetBrains Mono. State changes snap in two frames, the way a breaker throws, and the only continuous motion is current flowing along energised feeders. The world rejects the category default for energy dashboards: tiled KPI cards, donut charts, gradient fills and neon glow.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two themes, one role set: Daylight (navy chrome, light content) by default, Dusk (slate) on request.",
      "1px seams, flush panels; no cards.",
      "Blue accent for action and selection; a fixed hue per load type; amber only for waste; red/green only as panel lamps.",
      "Archivo for words, JetBrains Mono tabular for every figure.",
      "Switchgear drawn in SVG; line weight encodes kW.",
      "Two-step snaps for state; dash-flow for live current; nothing fades."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Source Rule",
        "body": "Every colour comes from a role in `theme.ts`. Markup uses role utilities (panel-*, chrome-*, accent, waste, lamp, chart, cat-*); SVG and chart code reads hex from `useTheme()`. A hardcoded hex does not switch themes and is a defect.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Action Blue Rule",
        "body": "Blue means \"act here\" or \"this is selected\": accent fill for the one primary action, accent border plus a 10% accent fill plus accent text for a pressed choice. Blue is never a load type's meaning in a control, never decoration, never waste.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Signal Rule",
        "body": "Amber means power into an empty room and nothing else. It never marks a hover, a primary action or a \"warning\" of another kind.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Panel-Lamp Rule",
        "body": "Red is energised and green is tripped (Indian switchgear convention). Never use green as \"good\" or red as \"bad\" outside that meaning, except error text and destructive actions where red already reads as live danger.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Load-Colour Rule",
        "body": "A load type has one colour everywhere it appears, and only the four controllable categories get one. Category labels stay in ink for contrast; the colour sits on the dot and a 14% tint.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Every figure (kW, kWh, °C, times, counts, room codes) is JetBrains Mono with tabular numerals. Words never are.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Label-Is-The-Heading Rule",
        "body": "The uppercase label is the panel's actual heading and carries its live count or value. It is never stacked as an ornamental line above another heading.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flush Panel Rule",
        "body": "Panels meet at 1px seams. If a block needs separation, add a seam, not a shadow, a gap or a card.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** take every colour from a `theme.ts` role so both Daylight and Dusk stay correct; SVG and charts read hex from `useTheme()`.",
      "**Do** separate every panel, row and region with a 1px Seam and let panels sit flush.",
      "**Do** set every figure in JetBrains Mono with tabular numerals, and put its unit in muted Archivo beside it.",
      "**Do** mark action and selection with Action Blue: accent fill for the primary action, accent border + 10% fill + accent text for a pressed choice.",
      "**Do** give each load type its own colour everywhere it appears (AC, Fans, Lights, AV), keep IT/appliances/other neutral, and keep category labels in ink.",
      "**Do** reserve Feeder Amber for power into an empty room, everywhere it appears.",
      "**Do** use the panel-lamp code: Lamp Red for energised, Lamp Green for tripped.",
      "**Do** snap state changes in two steps (90ms, steps(2, end)) and keep current flow as the only continuous motion, stilled under reduced motion.",
      "**Do** label simulated and estimated values in place (SIMULATED, ESTIMATED tags; \"· simulated\" beside timestamps).",
      "**Do** draw domain objects as SVG switchgear and use line weight, dash and lamp state to carry meaning."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** hardcode hex values in markup, SVG or charts; they will not switch theme.",
      "**Don't** use cards, glows, gradient fills or decorative shadows; the modal drop is the only shadow. Drawn patterns (the 20px drafting grid, the lunch hatch) are linework, not gradients, and stay allowed.",
      "**Don't** tile KPIs as cards or show proportions as donut charts; KPIs live in a seamed strip.",
      "**Don't** use amber, red or green as general-purpose accent, success or warning colours, and don't use Action Blue for anything but action and selection.",
      "**Don't** put category label text in the category colour; colour sits on the dot and the tint.",
      "**Don't** fade or ease state changes; nothing fades in this world.",
      "**Don't** round controls beyond 3px; only lamps, status and load-type dots are circles and only category chips are pills.",
      "**Don't** stack an uppercase label above a heading as an eyebrow; the label is the heading."
    ]
  }
}